c42c737b755d79535e5455e0439ee74f648a4a0bf.png)\nfind $\\log D.$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156969 1100 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}100 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind $\\log D$." 13194139535978 false] [17592186156969 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f(λ_{n})\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log D &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\log (100) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\log (1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\log (10)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 false] [17592186156969 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n,$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/fd9855d724ad4cac8d8f5f93aa81455aea3f0848c744b02fbafc2987054dc7d7.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{log}⁡(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{log}⁡(10)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156970 1100 "Find $f\\left(J_4(1) \\right)$ for $f(x)=2^x$." 13194139535978 false] [17592186156970 1100 "Find $f\\left(J_4(1) \\right)$ for $f(x)=2^x.$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156970 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2^{x}\n$$\n\n$n=4$, and $\\lambda=1$.\n\nSince $n=4$, we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 2^{x}\\ln 2 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(J_{4}(1)) &= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}f^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2^{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 false] [17592186156970 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda),$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/738039128c2eef5b848f6114c6008188456705206879411c9dab82e919bc18b5.png)\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x) = 2^{x},$ $n=4,$ and $\\lambda=1.$\n\nSince $n=4,$ we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{4}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & {2}^{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156971 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/17b4d68be43e4a4eb895ebbe623b713f1a808153424dd221b7497579d666b00a.png)\n\nGiven that $V = PJP^{-1},$ find $\\sin\\left(\\pi V\\right).$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156971 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $V = PJP^{-1}$, find $\\sin\\left(\\pi V\\right)$." 13194139535978 false] [17592186156971 1145 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S$, where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = S \\cdot f(A) \\cdot S^{-1}\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sin(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nand the given matrix $V$, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\pi V) = \\sin\\left(\\pi{PJP^{-1}}\\right) = P \\cdot (\\sin(\\pi J)) \\cdot P^{-1}\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $-3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\pi \\cos \\pi x\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (πV) &= P \\cdot (\\sin (πJ)) \\cdot P^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ π & - π\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 false] [17592186156971 1145 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = S \\cdot f(A) \\cdot S^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x) = \\sin(\\pi x)$ and the given matrix $V,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\pi V) = \\sin\\left(\\pi{PJP^{-1}}\\right) = P \\cdot (\\sin(\\pi J)) \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $-3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/a8ad3d4a15a5a8e7307fdd6449d8086d6c9c7e656caea877d41f021c7d8584cc.png)\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is $f'(x) = \\pi \\cos \\pi x.$ So, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/9af57a3b00b9c9e64508c119ae77690904bdb8c30b124b8165d4280149361bad.png)\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/ff9333784a9cdd38f510398b48e485764eac400f56c2cc9f2f1a18e5138845d8.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi V) & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi J))\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & -\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & -\\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156972 1100 "Let $J_n\\left(\\lambda\\right)$ denote the $n \\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda$ on the main diagonal. Then,  find\n$e^{2\\left(J_2(1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_2(3)\\right)}.$" 13194139535978 true] [17592186156972 1100 "Let $J_n\\left(\\lambda\\right)$ denote the $n \\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda$ on the main diagonal. Then, find\n$e^{2\\left(J_2(1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_2(3)\\right)}$." 13194139535978 false] [17592186156972 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2(J_{2}(1)⊕J_{2}(3))} &= e^{2(\\begin{bmatrix}J_{2}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & J_{2}(3)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2(J_{2}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{2(J_{2}(1))}⊕e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For $J_2(1)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=1$. Since $n=2$, we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n$f(x)|= e^{2x}; f^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}$\nSo, we get\n$f(J_{2}(1))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 1} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 1}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} \\\\ 0 & e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- For $J_2(3)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=3$. Since $n=2$, we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n$f(J_{2}(3))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(3) & f^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 3} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 3}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535978 false] [17592186156972 1145 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda),$ we have\n![](/home/j